# Instructions

At checks.{{ name }}, is the preceding reporting/sentence frame already syntactically complete OUTSIDE its finished quotation?

Use complete local_raw_evidence and preceding_source_index to locate the nearest genuine SAME-PAGE preceding main-text block. Delimiter cues and immediate_paragraph_hint are fallible locators, not a confirmed predecessor. Exclude ancillary material and do not invent missing text.

This checks a property of the LEFT block. Do not evaluate whether the target opening is well written or complete. Missing OCR characters in a new target do not create an unfinished left word or clause.

A reporting verb can have its complete object supplied by the finished quoted utterance already present in that block. After that complete reporting sentence, subsequent narration need not be its obligatory complement. Do not judge the new target as an object that the left frame is still missing merely because it begins oddly.

A finished quote does NOT automatically finish an outer conditional or subordinate clause. If the surrounding frame still requires its main clause or another obligatory component after the quote, answer FALSE. Distinguish that outside dependency from a fully completed reporting sentence.

Do not require prose to continue in one paragraph for topical coherence, and do not repair author wording or missing OCR. If the actual outside frame cannot be determined from the supplied text, answer FALSE.

# True

The outside reporting/grammatical frame is complete; it is not awaiting a mandatory clause or complement supplied by the target.

# False

An outside-quotation grammatical dependency remains, or the complete outside frame is not established.
